  <h2>The system: there is and there are</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>The noun after the verb decides the form. One thing: <span class="ex">there is</span>. More than one: <span class="ex">there are</span>.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th></th><th>singular</th><th>plural</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>positive</b></td><td>There is a desk.</td><td>There are two desks.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>negative</b></td><td>There isn’t a desk.</td><td>There aren’t any desks.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>question</b></td><td>Is there a desk?</td><td>Are there any desks?</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note-line"><span class="ex">There’s</span> is the usual spoken form of <span class="ex">there is</span>. Use <span class="ex">there are</span> for plural nouns. Short answers: <span class="ex">Yes, there is. / No, there aren’t.</span></p>

  <h2>Get to the root: introduce something into the scene</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p><span class="ex">There is / there are</span> answers the basic question <strong>“What exists or is available here?”</strong> It is an introducing structure. The listener does not need to know about the thing before you mention it.</p>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Existence first, location second</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">There is a café on this street.</span> introduces a café into the conversation. <span class="ex">The café is on this street.</span> identifies the location of a café that both people already know about.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><span class="ex">There’s a problem.</span> = I am introducing the problem.</li>
      <li><span class="ex">The problem is in this file.</span> = We already know which problem; now I locate it.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h3>“There” has a grammar job</h3>
  <p>In this pattern, <span class="ex">there</span> does not usually mean “in that place.” It holds the subject position so English can introduce new information after the verb. Compare <span class="ex">There is a bank there</span>: the first <span class="ex">there</span> builds the structure; the second one really means “in that place.”</p>

  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>New thing or known thing?</h3>
    <p>A speaker often uses <span class="ex">there is / there are</span> to bring a thing into the conversation. After both people know which thing it is, the speaker can use <span class="ex">it</span> or the noun with <span class="ex">the</span>.</p>
    <p><span class="ex">There’s a pharmacy on Oak Street. It’s beside the bank. The pharmacy closes at 8.</span></p>
    <p>The first sentence introduces the pharmacy. The next two sentences talk about that known pharmacy. This is a meaning choice, not simply a choice between two forms of <span class="ex">be</span>.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>The noun controls is or are</h3>
  <p>Look forward to the first noun after the verb. One countable thing or an uncountable substance takes <span class="ex">is</span>; plural things take <span class="ex">are</span>.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>noun idea</th><th>form</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>one thing</td><td>there is</td><td>There is <b>a message</b>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>uncountable amount</td><td>there is</td><td>There is <b>some water</b>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>more than one</td><td>there are</td><td>There are <b>three messages</b>.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note-line">In quick informal speech, you may hear <span class="ex">There’s two people outside</span>. Careful standard English uses <span class="ex">There are two people outside</span>, so that is the form to build first.</p>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Sound natural: link the structure and stress the new thing</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">There is / there are</span> introduces information, so the new noun usually carries the strongest beat: <span class="ex">There’s a <strong>CAFÉ</strong> nearby</span> and <span class="ex">There are two <strong>WIN</strong>dows</span>. The structure is lighter; the thing you are introducing is clearer.</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>careful form</th><th>natural spoken rhythm</th><th>focus</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>There is a problem.</td><td>There’s-a problem.</td><td><b>problem</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td>There are two seats.</td><td>There-are two seats.</td><td><b>two seats</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Is there a bank?</td><td>Is-there-a bank?</td><td><b>bank</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Are there any tables?</td><td>Are-there-any tables?</td><td><b>tables</b></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p><span class="ex">There’s</span> is a common spoken and written contraction. English normally writes <span class="ex">there are</span> as two words, although speakers make <span class="ex">are</span> very short and link it to <span class="ex">there</span>. Linking does not change the grammar. Keep <span class="ex">are</span> for plural nouns, and use the full careful rhythm when the listener needs extra clarity.</p>
  </div>

  <section data-lesson-extension="Tutor-read there is and there are listening check">
