  <h2>Questions, negatives, and useful answers</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Because <span class="ex">is / are</span> is the working verb, it moves before <span class="ex">there</span> in a question. It also carries <span class="ex">not</span> in a negative. No <span class="ex">do / does</span> is needed.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>question</th><th>short answer</th><th>natural extra information</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Is there a restroom?</td><td>Yes, there is.</td><td>It’s near the entrance.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Are there any seats?</td><td>No, there aren’t.</td><td>But there are some outside.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div class="note">
    <h3>Articles and quantity still matter</h3>
    <p>Use <span class="ex">a/an</span> with one countable noun: <span class="ex">There is a pharmacy</span>. Use a number or <span class="ex">some</span> with plural nouns. In ordinary questions and negatives, <span class="ex">any</span> is common: <span class="ex">Are there any pharmacies? There aren’t any nearby.</span></p>
    <p><span class="ex">No</span> gives the same zero quantity in another natural pattern: <span class="ex">There is no parking lot</span> and <span class="ex">There are no free seats</span>. Do not combine the two negative patterns: use <span class="ex">There aren’t any seats</span> or <span class="ex">There are no seats</span>.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="practice" data-answer-drill data-success-message="Correct. Each answer matches the following noun, carries the negative when required, or moves before there to form the stated question.">
    <h3>Drill 2: type is, are, isn’t, aren’t, Is, or Are</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Find the noun after the verb, then choose its form. Questions start with the verb.</p>
